SQL Injection:

	Detect: Monitor logs for unusual SQL patterns or errors.

	Contain: Block or throttle the IPs associated with the injection attempts.

	Analyze: Check the logs to identify the exact nature and source of the injection.

	Eradicate: Apply patches to fix the vulnerability (e.g., use parameterized queries or prepared statements).

	Recover: If data was affected, restore from backups.

	Review: After mitigation, conduct a post-mortem to determine root cause and prevention measures.

Unauthorized Access:

	Detect: Alerts triggered by failed login attempts or abnormal access patterns.

	Contain: Disable the user account involved, lock out the source IP.

	Analyze: Identify how access was gained and assess the scope.

	Eradicate: Implement stronger authentication mechanisms (e.g., Multi-Factor Authentication).

	Recover: Reset credentials, restore compromised data from backups.

	Review: Conduct a security audit to assess vulnerabilities.

Privilege Escalation:

	Detect: Detect unusual user behavior or privilege elevation.

	Contain: Revoke privileges for the affected account.

	Analyze: Investigate the root cause (e.g., system misconfiguration, unpatched vulnerability).

	Eradicate: Patch system vulnerabilities and reconfigure privileges.

	Recover: Restore access to legitimate users.

	Review: Conduct a security assessment to prevent future privilege escalation.

3. Communication Strategies

Internal Communication:

	Define who needs to be informed (e.g., IT staff, security team, management).

	Use encrypted communication channels (e.g., Slack, Microsoft Teams, etc.) for internal communication during the incident.

	Establish a centralized communication hub where all updates and actions can be logged in real-time.

	External Communication:

	If necessary, notify external stakeholders (e.g., customers, partners) in case of a breach.

	Prepare pre-written communication templates for breach disclosures, as per compliance requirements (e.g., GDPR, CCPA).

4. Post-Incident Review

	After resolving the incident, conduct a post-mortem or lessons learned session to:

	Review the effectiveness of the response.

	Identify any missed steps or areas for improvement.

	Adjust and improve playbooks, logging, and alerting procedures.

	Share findings with the team to improve future incident handling.
